## D-A — Flip Documents/other to `has_expiry=True` (observed-date suppressed, reuse `expiry_date`, no new `DateType`)

(a) Strongest case against, with evidence: the flip builds exactly what the blueprint forbids and then mislabels it. The date rule states: "Categories with no applicable expiry concept (clothing, tools, most documents) simply have no expiry assertion — the system must never force a date onto a category that doesn't logically need one, and must never fabricate a plausible-sounding date when none is visible" (`inception/generic_asset_catalog_expiry_tracker_blueprint.md:396`). `classify_asset` after the flip writes the placeholder unconditionally for every document with no observed date — `expiry_assertion = _write_assertion(db, asset, EXPIRY_FIELD, {"status": "unknown"}, "deterministic", "needs_evidence", None)` plus `_manual_task(db, asset)` (`backend/app/plugins/expiry_tracker.py:436-440`) — i.e. every keepsake, letter, and sentimental item classified as Documents/other gains a date record and an open manual-entry task by construction. The refinement suppresses the OCR proposal but keeps the forcing. The reuse then mislabels: `parse_manual_entry` defaults `date_type` to `DateType.EXPIRY_DATE` (`expiry_tracker.py:280-282`), the `DateType` enum has no validity/reminder member (`expiry_tracker.py:39-45`), and downstream readers render that value in expiry language — engine bucket `"expired"` (`expiry_engine.py:72-80`), analytics `"expired"` / `"Expired"` / `expired_untouched` / "ACTIVE asset whose active expiry_date < as_of" (`analytics/service.py:150,244-245,312,330`), reminders keyed off tier/category with no document exception (`reminders/service.py:62-67`). A passport renewal becomes an "expired" asset. Further, the windows themselves are `DECLARED UNCALIBRATED` (`expiry_tracker.py:122-126`, G-A9): Household = "one basic window," Documents = "the mode name's own 60/30 pair" — so the flip ships real reminders on invented numbers. It also reverses a shipped, tested contract: SG-073 kept `has_expiry=False` as faithful to blueprint §9.1 "reminder-only" (`SG-073_report.md:54,57,79`) and the pinning test asserts `documents_entry` is `422 "non-perishable"` (`test_plugin_expiry.py:159`); A1 requires breaking that test, not extending it.

(b) One unverified premise, and how to check: that every item the owner will put in Documents/other logically needs a deadline date (i.e. the owner's documents are passports/warranties, not keepsakes/letters — the blueprint says "most documents" have no concept). Check without writing: take a read-only census of the live household's existing `documents_other`-classifiable items (the SG-240 G5 pattern: 1 household, 7 ACTIVE assets, 1 accepted `food_beverages` — `SG-240_report.md:30`), or ask the owner to name two real documents they intend to track; a single keepsake/letter in the set falsifies the premise. The current record shows zero documented documents, so the premise has never been exercised.

(c) Settling observation: read the blueprint's category table (`:383-385` — Documents "reminder-only" vs Non-perishable "no expiry field applicable") alongside a live `documents_other` item list. If the owner's set is exclusively renewal-deadline documents and keepsakes are shown to live under `non_perishable`, the forcing objection dissolves and A1 stands (with the observed-date suppression). If any keepsake, letter, or photo belongs in Documents/other, A1 as worded falls and the honest namings (A4 `reminder_only` flag) or date-less close (A3) win — no implementation detail can rescue a scope error.

## D-B — Classification control on the asset detail page via served taxonomy and existing route, no `If-Match`

(a) Strongest case against, with evidence: the route is version-blind while the page pretends versions matter, and the page has no taxonomy plumbing. The classify route performs no version check (`backend/app/api/v1/plugins.py:125-153` — no `If-Match`, no `version`), unlike the asset PATCH which enforces `If-Match` against `asset.version` with 409 on mismatch (`backend/app/api/v1/assets.py:425-431`) and displays "Uses If-Match: {asset.version} for optimistic concurrency" (`AssetDetailPage.tsx:396`, mutation at `:286-294`). Last-writer-wins is therefore not "visible and re-doable": a stale tab overwriting the category silently re-triggers the C1/D-A expiry side effects (placeholder or preserved date) the loser never saw, and the audit row for classification (`expiry_tracker.py:359-368`) does not restore the lost date. Separately, the control assumes data the page never loads: the frontend has no classify wrapper (`client.ts` exports only `enterManualExpiry` at `:231-241`, `recordOpenedDate` at `:308`, `clearOpenedDate` at `:316`) and the pack records "a grep for `classify` over `frontend/src` returns no hit" (context pack:166); the asset page imports no taxonomy hook while the Expiry page does (`ExpiryPage.tsx:96` `useTaxonomy`). The taxonomy it would depend on is hand-mirrored — "Every category mirrors what `expiry_tracker.CATEGORIES` enforces" (`descriptor.py:126-131`) — with aliasing the UI cannot see (`_CATEGORY_ALIASES`, `expiry_tracker.py:171-187`): a drifted UI offers a category the backend rejects (422 via `canonical_category`) with no recovery path designed, and the empty-taxonomy case (PG-SC-07, cf. `drawer.test.tsx:301`) has no specified control state.

(b) One unverified premise, and how to check: that the served taxonomy's `active` flags are a safe UI source of truth. Check by diffing `CATEGORIES` slugs/active (`expiry_tracker.py:135-169`) against `EXPIRY_TRACKER_TAXONOMY` ids/active (`descriptor.py:138-174`) and the served shape (`assets.py:48-66`), then round-tripping each served active id through `canonical_category` (`expiry_tracker.py:216-229`); any served-but-rejected id, or enforceable-but-unserved slug, falsifies it. `opinion` on stakes: I judge a stale overwrite here as more than negligible because the overwrite silently moves the expiry record, not just the label.

(c) Settling observation: attempt two concurrent classifications of one asset from two stale page states against the real route and read back `GET /v1/assets/{id}` assertions plus the audit trail. If the loser's category and its date consequences are both reconstructible and re-doable through the same control with no silent date loss, the no-`If-Match` position stands. If the second write silently discards the first writer's category or date with no UI signal, the pick must gain `If-Match` (or a pre-write version read) — B1 as worded falls.

## D-C — In `classify_asset`, skip both placeholder and observed-date proposal when an active expiry exists; reverse move into `non_perishable` keeps the date (today's behaviour, tested and reported)

(a) Strongest case against, with evidence: C1 preserves bytes, not meaning, and the codified reverse move traps the date. Skipping both branches on any active assertion (`expiry_tracker.py:434-451` guarded by `_active_assertion`, which excludes only `superseded`/`rejected` at `:322-332`, so `proposed` counts as active) carries a `use_by` bleach date onto a passport with no re-prompt and no `date_type` cross-check (`canonical_date_type` accepts any member at `:231-237`); `compute_status` then tiers it purely on the accepted `EXPIRY_FIELD` plus the new slug with no category/`date_type` validation (`expiry_engine.py:262-282`), so the wrong-typed date immediately drives 60/30 reminders. The reverse move is worse: classifying into `non_perishable` executes no expiry write at all (`:434-451` whole block skipped), the old accepted date stays active, `compute_status` keeps reporting it (no `has_expiry` gate on the read path at `:256-282`), the D-D form hides for `tier_defaults == {}` (`Category.profile` at `:85-93`), and `store_manual_expiry` refuses the asset as "non-perishable" (`expiry_tracker.py:608-610`, pinned by `test_plugin_expiry.py:349-363`). The kept date is thus visible in Expiry/reminders but uneditable and unclearable through the app — and D-D explicitly ships no clear this slice. A `proposed`-active asset has a further limbo: skipping the placeholder branch also skips `_manual_task`, so no task ever prompts the accept that the engine requires (`engine` tiers only `accepted` at `:262-268`; `proposed` is reported unresolved at `:283-290`).

(b) One unverified premise, and how to check: that a carried-over date is semantically valid under the new category. Check with a dated-food → documents/household reclassification fixture: classify, enter a `use_by`/`best_before` date, reclassify, then read back the `EXPIRY_FIELD` `date_type` and the `compute_status` tier/bucket. If `date_type` survives unchanged across a semantic boundary (bleach → passport) and still tiers, the premise is false. No such cross-category fixture exists in the named tests.

(c) Settling observation: run the reverse-move fixture the pick promises — dated asset → `non_perishable` — then read `GET /v1/assets/{id}` assertions, `compute_status` rows, and attempt a manual correction. If the date still tiers while the correction path 422s and no form shows, the trap is confirmed and the pick must define the reverse move (supersede, refuse, or clear) rather than bless today's behaviour. If `compute_status` excludes dates under dateless classifications, the trap dissolves and C1 stands as worded.

## D-D — Show the form whenever the classification profile has `tier_defaults`; read the active plugin `EXPIRY_FIELD` (not bare/superseded); pre-fill accepted/proposed; Save writes accepted; no clear

(a) Strongest case against, with evidence: the gate predicate is not evaluable from served data, the "active" read is ambiguous against the served ordering, and no-clear makes mistakes irreversible. The served taxonomy carries only `notification` mode names, not numbers (`assets.py:52-60` serves `id/name/active/notification/opened_date_tracking/chat`; modes defined at `descriptor.py:40-49`; documents served as `"long-lead-60-30"` at `:162-167`). The frontend therefore cannot evaluate "profile has `tier_defaults`" without hardcoding a mode→windows map (duplicating `CATEGORIES`) or a new endpoint/shape — neither designed. Meanwhile the current finds ignore state — `assertions.find((item) => item.field_path.endsWith("expiry_date"))` (`AssetDetailPage.tsx:330`; `ExpiryEntryForm.tsx:18`) — over a collection the backend serves unfiltered and ordered only by `field_path` (`_asset_to_dict` iterates all `asset.assertions` with no state filter at `assets.py:131-144`; relationship `order_by="Assertion.field_path"` at `models/asset.py:40-44`), so intra-field order among accepted/superseded/proposed rows sharing `plugin:expiry-tracker/expiry_date` is unspecified and `find` is nondeterministic; the test fixture itself uses a different prefix (`plugin:expiry_tracker.expiry_date`, `ExpiryEntryForm.test.tsx:8`), showing `endsWith` fragility. "Active" is then ambiguous between `_active_assertion` (latest non-superseded/rejected, `expiry_tracker.py:322-332`) and the engine's accepted-only (`expiry_engine.py:106-116`), so pre-filling a `proposed` OCR date and letting Save launder it to `accepted` bypasses review — for non-documents the D-A suppression does not apply. Save itself is version-blind (`plugins.py:175-216`, no `If-Match`), and with the form now always shown for dated assets there is no dirty-check against accidental overwrite. Finally, D4 parity is broken by construction: opened dates ship Edit *and* Clear via a null active assertion (`OpenedCountdownSection.tsx:147-164`; `clear_opened_date` at `expiry_tracker.py:531-554`), while D1 ships Save-only — a mistaken document date, forced by D-A and preserved by D-C, has no UI path back to dateless.

(b) One unverified premise, and how to check: that the frontend can distinguish "classified with windows" from "dateless/unclassified" using data it already has. Check by reading a live `GET /v1/taxonomy` response and a `GET /v1/assets/{id}` body: if taxonomy carries no `tier_defaults` numbers and the asset assertions carry no profile, the D1 gate as worded is unimplementable and must be reworded (e.g. gate on `notification !== "none"`). `opinion` on the superseded-rows half: the pack framed it as uncertain, but `_asset_to_dict` plus the `order_by` prove superseded rows are served — that half of D1's motivation is verified, not assumed.

(c) Settling observation: render the form against an asset with one accepted plus one superseded `EXPIRY_FIELD` row and record which record pre-fills, then Save without editing and read the resulting review state. If the pre-fill is the superseded/dead record, or an untouched Save writes a new accepted row, D1 as specified is unsafe and must add an explicit active-selector (`_active_assertion` semantics, latest-wins) plus a dirty-guard; if it deterministically pre-fills the live active record and no-op Saves write nothing (cf. `_write_assertion` dedupe at `:344-348`), the read half stands and only the gate wording and the missing clear remain.

